=== GATE ===
Gate run with all consumer-cleanup edits applied AND both migration
files on disk. The transcript is the literal output the gate produced
on this run, captured immediately before the working-tree revert:

  === GATE ===
  Residual fleet_telemetry_subscriptions consumers remain -- step 2 incomplete:
  internal/api/fleet_telemetry_error_handler.go:257:// fleet_telemetry_subscriptions-derived health indicator with this
  internal/api/fleet_telemetry_handler.go:24:// fleet_telemetry_subscriptions table query with package-derived state
  internal/api/fleet_telemetry_handler.go:43:// fleet_telemetry_subscriptions table query (phase-42 ADR-004 #2).
  EXIT=1
  STATUS=BLOCKED

The three lines that trip the gate are PURE COMMENTS in files that
predecessor prompt 0068 added to document why the new
FleetTelemetryHandler / FleetTelemetryErrorHandler deliberately do NOT
query the legacy table. They contain no SQL and no Go references to
the dropped repo or the dropped struct -- only the literal string
"fleet_telemetry_subscriptions" inside a `//` comment.

The gate's residualRefs check at gate step #2 is unanchored:

  git --no-pager grep -nE 'fleet_telemetry_subscriptions|FleetSubscriptionRepo|NewFleetSubscriptionRepo' \
      -- '*.go' ':!*_test.go' ':!internal/database/migrations/'

It has no word boundaries, no comment stripping, and no per-file
allowlist beyond the test-file and migration-directory excludes. It
therefore cannot distinguish:
  (a) a SQL fragment like `INSERT INTO fleet_telemetry_subscriptions ...`
      (which IS what the gate is meant to catch and which Step 2 of this
      prompt successfully removes), from
  (b) a Go comment like `// fleet_telemetry_subscriptions table query ...`
      (which is documentation of WHY the new code does not query the
      legacy table, and which is correct work product from prompt 0068).

This is a STRUCTURAL CONTRADICTION inside the prompt as written:

  - The allowed-files whitelist (gate's git status check, lines toward
    the bottom of the gate) restricts edits to the six files listed in
    the prompt's "Allowed files to change" section. Neither
    fleet_telemetry_handler.go nor fleet_telemetry_error_handler.go is
    in that list.

  - The residualRefs check (gate step #2, before the git status check)
    matches the literal string in those two files' comments and exits
    with BLOCKED before the git status check runs.

  - Therefore: editing those files to remove the literal string would
    cause the git status check to BLOCK with "unrelated drift", and NOT
    editing them causes the residualRefs check to BLOCK with "consumer
    cleanup incomplete". There is no path through the gate within the
    allowed-files list as currently scoped.

Per Honesty Covenant clause #1 ("No red-as-green") and clause #2 ("No
scope narrowing - run the exact gate command, no subsets"), this is
BLOCKED. The gate as written cannot pass without either:

  - expanding the prompt's allowed-files list to include the two
    fleet_telemetry_*_handler.go files so the comments can be
    rewritten, OR

  - tightening the gate's residualRefs check so it ignores comment
    lines (e.g., `git grep -nE '^[^/]*\bfleet_telemetry_subscriptions\b'`
    or, more robustly, narrowing to SQL-context tokens like
    `INSERT INTO|FROM|UPDATE|DELETE FROM|JOIN` + the table name --
    the same pattern gate step #6 already uses for the banned-table
    list).

A FIXER PROMPT IS REQUIRED that does ONE of:

  Option F1 -- minimum scope expansion (RECOMMENDED):
    Add internal/api/fleet_telemetry_handler.go and
    internal/api/fleet_telemetry_error_handler.go to the allowed-files
    list, and add an explicit Step 2b that rewrites the three comment
    lines to NOT contain the literal string "fleet_telemetry_subscriptions".
    Suggested rewordings (lossless):
      :24  // legacy fleet-telemetry subscriptions table query with package-derived state
      :43  // legacy fleet-telemetry subscriptions table query (phase-42 ADR-004 #2).
      :257 // legacy fleet-telemetry-subscriptions-derived health indicator with this

  Option F2 -- gate hardening:
    Replace the residualRefs check (gate step #2) with the same
    SQL-context regex that gate step #6 uses for the banned-table list:
      git --no-pager grep -nE \
        "FROM\s+fleet_telemetry_subscriptions\b|INSERT\s+INTO\s+fleet_telemetry_subscriptions\b|UPDATE\s+fleet_telemetry_subscriptions\b|DELETE\s+FROM\s+fleet_telemetry_subscriptions\b|JOIN\s+fleet_telemetry_subscriptions\b" \
        -- '*.go' ':!*_test.go' ':!internal/database/migrations/'
    This would let the gate distinguish active SQL from documentation.
    The allowed-files list can stay exactly as-is.

  Option F3 -- combined:
    F1 + F2. Belt and suspenders. The comments get cleaned up AND the
    gate becomes structurally robust against future commenters who
    legitimately need to mention the dropped table by name.

Either F1 or F2 alone would unblock this prompt. F2 is the more
durable fix because it makes the gate's residualRefs check
consistent with its own banned-table check (which already correctly
ignores comments).

